Implemented Vercel Speed Insights for the Lumora project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Installed @vercel/speed-insights package
- Added `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0 as a dependency
- Used npm to install the package (matching the project's package manager)
- Updated package.json and package-lock.json accordingly

### 2. Integrated SpeedInsights component
- Modified `app/layout.tsx` to import the SpeedInsights component from `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Added the `<SpeedInsights />` component to the root layout, placed alongside the existing `<Analytics />` component
- Followed Next.js App Router best practices by adding it to the body element

### 3. Files Modified
- `app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights import and component
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new package dependency tree

## Implementation Details

The integration follows the official Vercel Speed Insights quickstart guide for Next.js 14+ projects:
- Used the Next.js-specific import path: `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Placed the component in the root layout to track performance across all pages
- Positioned alongside the existing Vercel Analytics component for consistency

## Verification Completed

✓ Build completed successfully with no errors
✓ ESLint passed with no warnings or errors
✓ TypeScript compilation successful
✓ All existing functionality preserved
✓ Lock files properly updated

## Notes

- The SpeedInsights component will automatically track Core Web Vitals and performance metrics
- Data collection begins once the app is deployed to Vercel and Speed Insights is enabled in the dashboard
- The integration is minimal and non-invasive, following the same pattern as the existing Analytics setup
- No configuration changes needed - works out of the box
